### The Allocation Trap in Hot Paths

While profiling a high-throughput telemetry ingestion pipeline, our telemetry showed excessive Gen0 GC collections. Tracing the allocations pointed directly to string manipulation methods inside our line-parsing loops. Every time we called `Substring` or split large payloads using standard string APIs, the runtime allocated new objects on the managed heap.

For systems processing gigabytes of continuous network or file data per second, these transient allocations degrade throughput and increase GC pause times.

### Before and After

**Suboptimal approach (allocates new strings on heap):**
```csharp
foreach (var line in text.Split('\n')) {
    var token = line.Substring(0, 4);
    Process(token);
}
```

**Optimized approach (zero heap allocations):**
```csharp
ReadOnlySpan<char> span = text.AsSpan();
foreach (var range in span.Split('\n')) {
    ReadOnlySpan<char> token = span[range].Slice(0, 4);
    Process(token);
}
```

### Why it matters
- **Zero Heap Allocations:** `ReadOnlySpan<T>` wraps existing memory buffers without duplicating data on the heap.
- **Pointer Arithmetic:** Slicing is performed via managed pointers, executing in constant O(1) time.
- **GC Relief:** Removing string fragments from the hot path drastically minimizes Gen0 garbage collection cycles.

### Watch out
`ReadOnlySpan<T>` is a ref struct, meaning it is restricted to the stack. You cannot store spans inside fields of standard classes, nor can you use them across `await` boundaries in asynchronous methods.

